RefineMesh's `--ensure-edge-size 2` ("force") applies the CGAL isotropic-remeshing
pass at *every* image scale of the coarse-to-fine loop, not only to the input mesh.
This scopes "force" to the first scale, which is the only scale the other two
values can reach.

The pass is `Mesh::EnsureEdgeSize` -> `PMP::isotropic_remeshing`, 10 iterations
(`Mesh.h:187` defaults `max_iters=50`, clamped by `MINF(max_iters, 10)`), followed
by a `Mesh::Clean` -- both single-threaded CGAL over a `Surface_mesh` copy of the
whole mesh, and both logging only on completion, so a long one is indistinguishable
from a hang.

Where it runs is decided by the guard at `SceneRefine.cpp:556`:

    if ((nEnsureEdgeSize == 1 && !bNoDecimation) || nEnsureEdgeSize > 1)

`fDecimateMesh` reaches `SubdivideMesh` only on the first scale -- every later one
is passed `1.f`, which sets `bNoDecimation` -- so at `0` (disabled) and at the
default `1` (auto) the pass already runs at most once, on the input. Only `2`
crosses into the later scales, where the mesh has just been subdivided against
images 4x larger in area per scale, and where `EnsureEdgeSize`'s own default
target of 2.25x the current mean edge length (`epsilonMin=-0.5`, `epsilonMax=-4`,
target `(0.5 + 4)/2` mean edges; confirmed by the `Ensured edge size` line of a
real run at 0.0620153 against a 0.0275624 mean) then remeshes coarser than what
that subdivision produced.

This matters here because pgs-recon's `coarsen` stage must pass `--decimate 1
--ensure-edge-size 2` (ADR 0009 s4): with its own decimation off, "force" is the
only value that reaches the pass at all, and upstream's tri-state has no value
meaning "force on the input only". So turning off the CGAL decimation the coarsen
stage replaces also, unavoidably, turns the remeshing pass on at the two later
scales, which no stock OpenMVS run performs -- with all defaults the pass runs
exactly once, at scale 0.

Behaviour for `--ensure-edge-size 0` and `1` is unchanged: the guard already
excluded the later scales for both. Only "force" changes, and it changes toward
what every other configuration already does.

The file's own `#define MESHOPT_ENSUREEDGESIZE 1 // 0 - at all resolution`
(`SceneRefine.cpp:42`) records the same intent: value 1 selects the first
resolution, 0 would select all of them. Not fixed upstream and not reported --
the behaviour is only reachable via "force", which upstream's own defaults do not
use. The CUDA path (`Scene::RefineMeshCUDA`) carries a duplicate of the loop and
the guard, and is patched identically; it is a separate code path in the same
binary, selected by --cuda-device.

--- a/libs/MVS/SceneRefine.cpp
+++ b/libs/MVS/SceneRefine.cpp
@@ -1312,7 +1312,12 @@
 		refine.ListVertexFacesPre();
 
 		// automatic mesh subdivision
-		refine.SubdivideMesh(nMaxFaceArea, nScale == 0 ? fDecimateMesh : 1.f, nCloseHoles, nEnsureEdgeSize);
+		// the edge-size pass conditions the mesh the optimizer starts from, so it
+		// belongs to the first scale only, like the decimation beside it: at the
+		// finer scales it remeshes what Subdivide has just built, from ~4x the
+		// faces per scale, and to a target coarser than that subdivision.
+		refine.SubdivideMesh(nMaxFaceArea, nScale == 0 ? fDecimateMesh : 1.f, nCloseHoles,
+			nScale == 0 ? nEnsureEdgeSize : 0);
 
 		// extract array of triangle normals
 		refine.ListVertexFacesPost();
--- a/libs/MVS/SceneRefineCUDA.cpp
+++ b/libs/MVS/SceneRefineCUDA.cpp
@@ -870,7 +870,9 @@
